## What this is

The short answer checklist is a short list of checks for the first paragraph of a page. It exists so that every short answer on the site is held to the same standard, whoever wrote it. The article on [how to write a short answer](/blog/how-to-write-a-short-answer) explains the reasons behind each check.

## The checks

| Check    | Pass                                  | Failure                                              |
| -------- | ------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| Length   | Forty to sixty words.                 | One sentence, or a paragraph longer than the screen. |
| Subject  | The subject is named in full.         | The text says it or this and the reader must guess.  |
| Result   | The first sentence states the result. | The first sentence gives background.                 |
| Pointers | No word points back at the page.      | The text says this page, below or above.             |

## When to run it again

Run the checks whenever the page changes in a way that moves its point. A new example does not need a new check. A new conclusion does. Search engines may still quote an old short answer, so an answer that no longer fits the page should be fixed before anything else. Read about the [plain language guide](https://digital.gov/guides/plain-language/) for the wider rules behind these checks.
